## Section 5 - Surveys & Feedback

3 surveys, all live. **No new responses since the last run.** All counts and most-recent-response dates unchanged: Newsletter feedback 3 (2025-02-03), Welcome/subscribe 73 (2026-04-16), Unsubscribe 5 (2026-07-20).

**Standing flag, fourth run:** the welcome/subscribe survey has had zero responses for **four months**, across roughly 32 new subscribers in that period. Set against Section 4's finding that the stream poll also sits at a 1.8% response rate, there is now a pattern worth naming: **two separate subscriber-input mechanisms are both returning near-zero.** That is more likely a serve/placement problem than two independent cases of disinterest. Confirm the welcome survey is actually presented on signup.

**Contributor / collaboration pool:** unchanged at **27 Yes plus 36 Maybe = 63 all-time**, of whom **only 4 have ever had a personal 1:1 touch** (the 17 Jun batch). Carried forward rather than re-derived, defensible again this run because there were no new survey responses that could have changed it. The dynamic Warm Contributors segment is **59 members**. The gap between "said yes" and "got contacted" is 59 people and has not narrowed in nine weeks.

**Warm-contributor tracker check** (`15_WARM_CONTRIBUTOR_TRACKER.md`, last updated 2026-07-16): Ahmed Elbendary remains REPLIED / HOT with an open owed item. The ~470-word bylined piece "The dashboard was green. The system was not." still has not shipped, now **nine weeks** since his 17 Jun reply. Per the tracker's own instruction, this keeps flagging until it ships. The tracker's recommended path is unchanged: Growth drafts the piece from his case 1 and sends for sign-off, rather than nudging again.

## Section 6 - Rising Themes

**Google Trends: UNAVAILABLE, fourth consecutive run.** `trends.google.com/trends/explore` for the fixed keyword set rendered further than in previous runs (the page chrome, the four query chips and the "Worldwide, Past 90 days" scope all came back as text) but **no interest data of any kind was extractable**. The comparison chart and the rising/breakout terms are canvas-rendered. This is now four reproducible failures out of four attempts. **Recommendation stands and hardens: the spec should retire the Trends step and make WebSearch the primary for Section 6.** It has never once produced data and costs a browser round-trip every run.

**WebSearch supplement (observability / SRE / AIOps, Aug 2026):**

1. **Agentic AI in observability** remains the dominant theme: AI agents ingesting observability data to accomplish operational goals, AIOps moving from alerting to proactive remediation.
2. **Predictive AIOps / "AIOps 2.0"**: forecasting failure and degradation from historical telemetry before user impact, framed as the 2026 paradigm shift from reactive firefighting.
3. **Telemetry data authenticity and provenance** confirmed for a second consecutive run, and now with more substance. The framing: as synthetic and machine-generated content blends into legitimate telemetry, teams cannot trust alerts, model accuracy or automated decisioning, which drives demand for built-in data provenance and integrity checks in monitoring pipelines.
4. **NEW this run: privacy-by-design and zero-trust observability.** Multiple 2026 sources frame privacy-by-design observability as a hard requirement rather than a nice-to-have for regulated industries, naming banks, healthcare, insurance and consumer tech as being pushed to treat telemetry with the same care as financial or health records. A complementary technical thread: integrating OpenTelemetry with a zero-trust workload identity manager to authenticate observability pipelines.

**Top emerging keywords for the SEO radar:**

1. **telemetry data authenticity / observability data provenance.** Carried from 08-10 and now corroborated. Still un-covered on the site, still sits directly on Allan's garbage-in-garbage-out arc (Ep 26's Hard Stop). The byte-size angle recommended last run, "how do you know your telemetry is telling the truth", is unchanged and still unbuilt.
2. **observability in regulated industries / privacy-by-design observability (NEW).** This one has an unusual amount of supporting evidence to act on: it is rising in the trade press, it maps onto Allan's banking and enterprise background, **and it is already appearing in his own Search Console data** as a live advisory-page query ("observability in banking", position 50.0, 1 impression). A commercial-intent brief here would target a term the advisory page already half-ranks for.

## Section 7 - Actions & Flags

**7a - Change control review (`04_CHANGE_LOG.md`).** The file uses Applied / Verified / Recommend status tags rather than the literal "Pending fix" / "Proposed" strings; surfacing the equivalent open items:

- **CHG-002 plus CHG-005:** still "Applied, spot-check pending". They were confirmed live via browser on 08-03 with page titles matching target exactly. **Fifth consecutive cycle** carrying them as pending with no reason left. Al to flip both to Verified.
- **CHG-016:** dormant "Metrics & Mayhem" publication (`pub_199a2744...`) still awaiting Al's delete in beehiiv.
- **CHG-014:** 10 of 11 staged Signal Drop hook-title / CTA drafts still awaiting publish (only #1 live).
- **CHG-014 residual:** the crossed slugs on "Control Beats Perfection" / "The Last Person in the Queue" remain unresolved and still need Al to confirm which body is which.
- **Metadata drift check** (live beehiiv meta vs `00_SEO_Metadata_Tracker.xlsx`): **not run again this session, fourth consecutive skip.** Flagging rather than skipping silently. Recommend it be either scheduled properly as its own job or formally dropped from the spec, because a check that is skipped four runs running is not a control.
- **Carried:** duplicate Signal Drop post "Your Role Changes Every Hour", two live post IDs, fourth run flagged.

**7b - Prioritised actions for this period:**

1. **Pull the advisory lever. It has now improved twice while sitting untouched.** Position 7.6, 19 impressions, 0 clicks. A title and meta-description pass on `/advisory` is the highest-leverage single edit on the board, it is the commercial page, and it was the number-two action last run too. Every week it waits is a week of page-one impressions converting at zero.
2. **Diagnose the bounce/duration collapse before adding traffic.** Bounce 38.22 to 41.45 to 44.59 to 48.59 across four runs; session duration 111.3s to 72.03s. Sessions are up 21% and money-page views are down 37%. More reach into a leakier site makes the leak bigger, and the LinkedIn evidence (56 sessions, 0 signups) says the landing experience is where it fails.
3. **Build the CTA path on the author pages, and de-duplicate the two URLs.** 26% of organic clicks at 23.4% and 13.6% CTR routing nowhere. Flagged 08-10, unchanged 08-17. Cheapest conversion win on the site and it stays cheap only while nobody else notices.
4. **Fix subscriber input, do not add more of it.** The welcome survey is four months dry and the stream poll has 10 answers from 558 subscribers. Confirm both are actually being served before building anything else that depends on subscriber-supplied data, because GR-08-03's segmentation is currently sitting on a 1.8% sample.
5. **Ship Ahmed Elbendary's bylined piece (nine weeks) and flip CHG-002 / CHG-005 to Verified (five cycles).** Both cost almost nothing and both have been carried longer than any other open item in this plan.

